1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:04,385
We hear a lot these days about how hard it is to

2
00:00:04,400 --> 00:00:07,905
get a developer job. When I talk to computer

3
00:00:07,920 --> 00:00:10,405
science students, one question comes up often: "Has

4
00:00:10,420 --> 00:00:12,930
AI made it harder to get hired as a developer?"

5
00:00:13,140 --> 00:00:16,290
AI really does write code very well now. A

6
00:00:16,460 --> 00:00:19,185
few prompts can produce a simple program or

7
00:00:19,200 --> 00:00:21,725
game. When an error occurs, you can paste the

8
00:00:21,740 --> 00:00:24,320
error message into AI and ask it to fix it.

9
00:00:25,050 --> 00:00:28,680
That can naturally lead to this thought: "Do I even

10
00:00:28,870 --> 00:00:31,560
need to be good at coding myself anymore?" But after

11
00:00:31,650 --> 00:00:35,175
working as a developer and teaching students

12
00:00:35,190 --> 00:00:38,100
programming, I find myself asking the opposite question.

13
00:00:38,370 --> 00:00:41,600
In the age of AI, why should a company hire a junior developer?

14
00:00:41,770 --> 00:00:44,875
More specifically, why should it hire a

15
00:00:44,890 --> 00:00:47,655
junior who can only make a program with AI?

16
00:00:47,670 --> 00:00:49,680
That is what I want to discuss today.

17
00:00:50,650 --> 00:00:53,215
First, I am not saying AI has had

18
00:00:53,230 --> 00:00:55,320
no effect on developer hiring.

19
00:00:55,570 --> 00:00:57,140
It clearly has.

20
00:00:57,330 --> 00:00:59,155
But consider the statement: "It is hard to find

21
00:00:59,170 --> 00:01:00,780
a job because AI is replacing developers."

22
00:01:01,070 --> 00:01:03,795
I do not think that one sentence is enough

23
00:01:03,810 --> 00:01:06,320
to explain the entire junior hiring problem.

24
00:01:06,710 --> 00:01:09,495
If you search for C++ developers on Korean job

25
00:01:09,510 --> 00:01:13,655
sites such as Saramin or JobKorea, you can still

26
00:01:13,670 --> 00:01:15,840
find a substantial number of job listings.

27
00:01:16,567 --> 00:01:18,857
Of course, we should not take that number at face

28
00:01:19,187 --> 00:01:22,692
value. It includes experienced positions. A listing

29
00:01:22,707 --> 00:01:25,932
that contains the keyword C++ does not necessarily

30
00:01:25,947 --> 00:01:28,217
mean a pure C++ role for a new graduate.

31
00:01:28,627 --> 00:01:31,032
So I am not saying, "There are lots of listings, so

32
00:01:31,047 --> 00:01:33,537
the hiring crisis is a lie." My question is different.

33
00:01:33,887 --> 00:01:35,737
Companies are still looking for developers.

34
00:01:35,887 --> 00:01:37,792
Why is it so hard for junior

35
00:01:37,807 --> 00:01:40,407
developers to get hired?

36
00:01:41,133 --> 00:01:43,458
Here I want to ask a somewhat uncomfortable

37
00:01:43,473 --> 00:01:46,598
question. Imagine a computer science student

38
00:01:46,613 --> 00:01:50,643
whose GPA is close to 4.0. They have studied C++.

39
00:01:50,753 --> 00:01:53,963
They have studied data structures and algorithms.

40
00:01:54,093 --> 00:01:56,418
They can even explain what a linked list

41
00:01:56,433 --> 00:01:59,178
is. But suppose we turn off the internet

42
00:01:59,193 --> 00:02:01,938
and AI, give them an empty project, and say,

43
00:02:01,953 --> 00:02:03,773
"Implement a linked list from scratch."

44
00:02:04,500 --> 00:02:05,705
What would happen?

45
00:02:05,720 --> 00:02:08,545
Or, to make it a little more complex, what if we

46
00:02:08,560 --> 00:02:11,250
said, "Build a simple Tetris game from scratch"?

47
00:02:11,500 --> 00:02:13,785
Within my own experience teaching students, more

48
00:02:13,800 --> 00:02:16,830
people get stuck here than you might expect.

49
00:02:16,980 --> 00:02:19,485
Of course, this is my personal teaching experience.

50
00:02:19,500 --> 00:02:21,165
I am not saying every computer

51
00:02:21,180 --> 00:02:22,750
science student is like this.

52
00:02:22,940 --> 00:02:24,425
But I think it reveals an

53
00:02:24,440 --> 00:02:25,765
extremely important distinction.

54
00:02:25,780 --> 00:02:27,225
Reading code and writing code

55
00:02:27,240 --> 00:02:28,980
are different abilities.

56
00:02:29,700 --> 00:02:32,950
I think programming is similar to learning English.

57
00:02:33,200 --> 00:02:34,985
Imagine someone who has studied

58
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:36,430
English grammar very hard.

59
00:02:36,580 --> 00:02:38,710
Show them a sentence and they can interpret it.

60
00:02:38,860 --> 00:02:40,745
They can find the subject and

61
00:02:40,760 --> 00:02:42,550
verb and explain the grammar.

62
00:02:42,780 --> 00:02:45,130
But then bring in an English speaker and say, "Have

63
00:02:45,320 --> 00:02:47,525
a conversation in English for the next 30 minutes."

64
00:02:47,540 --> 00:02:50,430
That becomes a completely different problem.

65
00:02:50,580 --> 00:02:52,525
Knowing English grammar and conversing

66
00:02:52,540 --> 00:02:54,550
in English are different things.

67
00:02:54,780 --> 00:02:56,290
Programming is similar.

68
00:02:56,480 --> 00:02:57,525
Show them linked list code,

69
00:02:57,540 --> 00:02:59,300
and they can explain it.

70
00:03:00,033 --> 00:03:01,798
They understand that there is a Node

71
00:03:01,813 --> 00:03:03,578
and a Next Pointer, and that the

72
00:03:03,593 --> 00:03:05,623
pointer points to the next node.

73
00:03:05,853 --> 00:03:08,398
But give them a blank screen and say, "Build it yourself."

74
00:03:08,413 --> 00:03:10,743
Now the situation changes.

75
00:03:10,953 --> 00:03:12,298
How do I define a Node?

76
00:03:12,313 --> 00:03:14,158
Where do I manage Head?

77
00:03:14,173 --> 00:03:15,483
How do I insert a node?

78
00:03:15,673 --> 00:03:17,838
How should the links change when I delete one?

79
00:03:17,853 --> 00:03:19,298
What if I delete the last node?

80
00:03:19,313 --> 00:03:21,303
When do I free the memory?

81
00:03:21,473 --> 00:03:24,263
They have to produce all of this from their own mind.

82
00:03:24,473 --> 00:03:26,798
I think this is programming proficiency, which is

83
00:03:26,813 --> 00:03:29,633
different from simply knowing programming syntax.

84
00:03:30,367 --> 00:03:32,952
Tell someone making Tetris for the

85
00:03:32,967 --> 00:03:34,732
first time, "Build Tetris," and

86
00:03:34,747 --> 00:03:36,437
they may not know where to begin.

87
00:03:36,827 --> 00:03:38,732
But someone who has built small programs or

88
00:03:38,747 --> 00:03:40,757
games themselves many times is different.

89
00:03:41,087 --> 00:03:42,492
When asked to build Tetris, they naturally

90
00:03:42,507 --> 00:03:44,917
start breaking the problem down.

91
00:03:45,307 --> 00:03:46,532
I will need input.

92
00:03:46,547 --> 00:03:48,012
I will need blocks.

93
00:03:48,027 --> 00:03:49,352
I will need a board.

94
00:03:49,367 --> 00:03:51,072
I will need collision detection.

95
00:03:51,087 --> 00:03:52,592
I will need rotation.

96
00:03:52,607 --> 00:03:54,657
I will need to check whether a row is complete.

97
00:03:54,967 --> 00:03:57,512
In what order should the game loop update everything?

98
00:03:57,527 --> 00:03:59,777
These questions begin to arise naturally.

99
00:04:00,767 --> 00:04:02,637
It is not because they memorized the code.

100
00:04:02,867 --> 00:04:04,452
They have repeatedly experienced turning

101
00:04:04,467 --> 00:04:06,757
a problem into a program structure.

102
00:04:06,867 --> 00:04:09,832
It is like someone who has practiced English conversation extensively:

103
00:04:09,847 --> 00:04:12,737
they do not solve a grammar exercise in their head before every sentence.

104
00:04:12,887 --> 00:04:16,127
Ultimately, programming also requires repeated practice.

105
00:04:16,850 --> 00:04:18,520
This is where AI enters the picture.

106
00:04:18,950 --> 00:04:21,475
Now a student building Tetris does not have to

107
00:04:21,490 --> 00:04:23,380
think through everything from beginning to end.

108
00:04:23,890 --> 00:04:27,695
They can ask AI, "Make Tetris in C++."

109
00:04:27,710 --> 00:04:30,675
Code appears. If it has a compilation

110
00:04:30,690 --> 00:04:33,035
error, they say, "Fix this error." If

111
00:04:33,050 --> 00:04:35,535
they need a new feature, they say,

112
00:04:35,550 --> 00:04:37,735
"Add a display for the next block."

113
00:04:37,750 --> 00:04:40,995
This means someone can create a fairly convincing

114
00:04:41,010 --> 00:04:43,875
result without much programming proficiency. But we

115
00:04:43,890 --> 00:04:46,540
should be careful not to misunderstand the point.

116
00:04:47,417 --> 00:04:49,102
I am not saying that using

117
00:04:49,117 --> 00:04:50,947
AI is itself a problem.

118
00:04:51,037 --> 00:04:52,747
I use AI.

119
00:04:52,837 --> 00:04:55,527
Experienced developers use AI too.

120
00:04:55,657 --> 00:04:57,762
The ability to use AI effectively will

121
00:04:57,777 --> 00:04:59,967
become extremely important for developers.

122
00:05:00,137 --> 00:05:01,577
So we need to change the question.

123
00:05:02,300 --> 00:05:03,745
Of course such a developer can have value.

124
00:05:03,760 --> 00:05:06,665
Being unable to make Tetris without AI does not

125
00:05:06,680 --> 00:05:09,010
automatically make someone worthless as a developer.

126
00:05:09,140 --> 00:05:11,785
If they can use AI to deliver what a company

127
00:05:11,800 --> 00:05:14,650
needs quickly, that is an ability too.

128
00:05:14,840 --> 00:05:18,545
But what matters is how they use AI. Imagine

129
00:05:18,560 --> 00:05:21,665
two developers. The first receives a

130
00:05:21,680 --> 00:05:25,020
requirement and passes it straight to AI.

131
00:05:25,750 --> 00:05:26,995
They receive code.

132
00:05:27,010 --> 00:05:28,595
An error occurs.

133
00:05:28,610 --> 00:05:31,015
They paste the error message into AI.

134
00:05:31,030 --> 00:05:32,335
AI fixes it.

135
00:05:32,350 --> 00:05:33,235
They run it.

136
00:05:33,250 --> 00:05:34,115
It works.

137
00:05:34,130 --> 00:05:34,800
They are done.

138
00:05:34,850 --> 00:05:37,375
The second person also uses AI.

139
00:05:37,390 --> 00:05:39,155
But first they break down the problem.

140
00:05:39,170 --> 00:05:41,715
They identify which systems need to change.

141
00:05:41,730 --> 00:05:43,195
They decide on a structure.

142
00:05:43,210 --> 00:05:45,695
They delegate the implementation they need to AI.

143
00:05:45,710 --> 00:05:48,555
They integrate the generated code with the existing code.

144
00:05:48,570 --> 00:05:50,975
When a problem occurs, they use a

145
00:05:50,990 --> 00:05:52,475
debugger and profiler to find the cause.

146
00:05:52,490 --> 00:05:54,455
They change the design if necessary.

147
00:05:54,470 --> 00:05:56,795
Then they use AI again.

148
00:05:56,810 --> 00:05:58,870
Both people used AI.

149
00:05:59,600 --> 00:06:02,085
Both might even develop much more

150
00:06:02,100 --> 00:06:03,930
slowly without AI than they do now.

151
00:06:04,280 --> 00:06:05,605
But from the company's perspective,

152
00:06:05,620 --> 00:06:07,510
the two do not have the same value.

153
00:06:08,250 --> 00:06:11,855
That is why I also disagree with the claim that

154
00:06:11,870 --> 00:06:14,775
"real developers must code without AI." That is not

155
00:06:14,790 --> 00:06:17,895
the important point. When AI is available, there

156
00:06:17,910 --> 00:06:21,080
is no need to write every repetitive line by hand.

157
00:06:21,310 --> 00:06:25,055
At work, we search, read official documentation

158
00:06:25,070 --> 00:06:28,275
and existing code, ask colleagues, and use AI.

159
00:06:28,290 --> 00:06:31,695
What matters is not how many lines we can write

160
00:06:31,710 --> 00:06:34,815
without AI. It is whether we can solve problems

161
00:06:34,830 --> 00:06:38,010
independently using tools, including AI.

162
00:06:38,733 --> 00:06:41,623
Someone whose productivity is increased by AI is

163
00:06:41,833 --> 00:06:44,798
different from someone who cannot even figure out

164
00:06:44,813 --> 00:06:47,213
how to turn a problem into a program without AI.

165
00:06:47,933 --> 00:06:50,658
Suppose a junior developer's job is to receive

166
00:06:50,673 --> 00:06:54,018
requirements, pass them to AI, generate code,

167
00:06:54,033 --> 00:06:56,818
ask AI again when an error occurs, and submit

168
00:06:56,833 --> 00:06:59,383
the result. A company might think the following.

169
00:06:59,693 --> 00:07:01,638
"Then why should we hire this person?" The

170
00:07:01,653 --> 00:07:04,683
company already has experienced developers.

171
00:07:04,893 --> 00:07:07,378
It has people who know the existing project,

172
00:07:07,393 --> 00:07:09,758
understand its systems, can break down

173
00:07:09,773 --> 00:07:12,013
problems, design solutions, and debug.

174
00:07:12,733 --> 00:07:15,038
Crucially, those experienced

175
00:07:15,053 --> 00:07:17,563
developers can use the same AI.

176
00:07:18,073 --> 00:07:21,118
They can analyze the problem, design the structure,

177
00:07:21,133 --> 00:07:24,398
use AI to speed up implementation, verify the

178
00:07:24,413 --> 00:07:28,178
generated code, and debug when problems occur.

179
00:07:28,193 --> 00:07:31,243
Finally, they can apply the result to the actual service.

180
00:07:31,613 --> 00:07:34,578
In this environment, the economic reason to hire

181
00:07:34,593 --> 00:07:37,118
several junior developers for simple implementation

182
00:07:37,133 --> 00:07:39,403
work may become weaker than it used to be.

183
00:07:40,333 --> 00:07:42,938
Let us talk about the game industry. You might

184
00:07:42,953 --> 00:07:45,718
think game development means making a game,

185
00:07:45,733 --> 00:07:48,658
releasing it, and finishing. But live service

186
00:07:48,673 --> 00:07:51,658
projects, including online and mobile games, are

187
00:07:51,673 --> 00:07:54,423
an important part of major Korean game companies.

188
00:07:54,653 --> 00:07:57,298
For these games, release is not the end. It is

189
00:07:57,313 --> 00:08:00,163
closer to the beginning. Development continues

190
00:08:00,373 --> 00:08:03,213
after launch. New characters and items are added.

191
00:08:03,933 --> 00:08:07,358
Content and events are added. Balance is

192
00:08:07,373 --> 00:08:10,443
adjusted. Servers and clients keep changing.

193
00:08:10,533 --> 00:08:13,038
Bugs appear after updates. When a live incident

194
00:08:13,053 --> 00:08:15,723
occurs, someone has to find its cause quickly.

195
00:08:15,953 --> 00:08:18,173
And this process repeats for years.

196
00:08:18,900 --> 00:08:21,065
Suppose you use AI to

197
00:08:21,080 --> 00:08:23,150
build an inventory system.

198
00:08:23,600 --> 00:08:26,390
For a personal project, you might be finished once it works.

199
00:08:26,860 --> 00:08:29,470
A live project is different.

200
00:08:29,800 --> 00:08:32,745
One day a designer says, "Please add an item-locking

201
00:08:32,760 --> 00:08:35,470
feature." At first it sounds very simple. But

202
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:39,030
in a real project, there is a lot to consider.

203
00:08:39,260 --> 00:08:40,285
What about selling?

204
00:08:40,300 --> 00:08:41,045
Dropping?

205
00:08:41,060 --> 00:08:41,905
Trading?

206
00:08:41,920 --> 00:08:42,985
Moving items to storage?

207
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:43,745
Upgrading?

208
00:08:43,760 --> 00:08:44,685
Equipping?

209
00:08:44,700 --> 00:08:45,865
Server persistence?

210
00:08:45,880 --> 00:08:47,345
The client UI?

211
00:08:47,360 --> 00:08:48,485
Network synchronization?

212
00:08:48,500 --> 00:08:51,220
Then, six months later, the requirement changes.

213
00:08:51,950 --> 00:08:54,655
"Allow locked items to be upgraded during certain

214
00:08:54,670 --> 00:08:57,215
events." Now you have to add another feature on

215
00:08:57,230 --> 00:08:59,975
top of the code you wrote before. In a live service,

216
00:08:59,990 --> 00:09:02,255
generating new code is not the only important

217
00:09:02,270 --> 00:09:04,615
ability. You also need to understand the existing

218
00:09:04,630 --> 00:09:07,310
system and safely build changes on top of it.

219
00:09:08,033 --> 00:09:11,163
Building a small project from scratch with AI is different

220
00:09:11,353 --> 00:09:14,918
from joining a huge codebase written by many developers over

221
00:09:14,933 --> 00:09:17,558
several years and being asked, "Find the cause of this bug."

222
00:09:17,573 --> 00:09:20,658
You have to find where the problem

223
00:09:20,673 --> 00:09:23,598
occurs. You need to understand why the

224
00:09:23,613 --> 00:09:26,183
existing code was written this way.

225
00:09:26,313 --> 00:09:28,378
You also need to consider how changing

226
00:09:28,393 --> 00:09:30,563
it will affect other systems.

227
00:09:30,673 --> 00:09:32,258
You have to implement new requirements

228
00:09:32,273 --> 00:09:34,273
without breaking existing features.

229
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:36,665
When an incident occurs in a running

230
00:09:36,680 --> 00:09:38,850
service, you must trace its cause.

231
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:41,425
You cannot simply say, "Let us ask

232
00:09:41,440 --> 00:09:44,110
AI to rebuild it from scratch."

233
00:09:44,300 --> 00:09:46,185
It is already running and connected

234
00:09:46,200 --> 00:09:47,850
to countless other systems.

235
00:09:48,140 --> 00:09:50,705
That is why live service development

236
00:09:50,720 --> 00:09:52,430
requires not just a Feature Creator,

237
00:09:52,640 --> 00:09:53,765
but also a Problem Solver

238
00:09:53,780 --> 00:09:56,120
and a System Maintainer.

239
00:09:56,850 --> 00:09:58,655
Consider junior developer A.

240
00:09:58,670 --> 00:10:00,455
With AI, they can make games

241
00:10:00,470 --> 00:10:02,580
and implement features.

242
00:10:02,750 --> 00:10:05,115
But without AI, they get stuck even

243
00:10:05,130 --> 00:10:07,140
on how to break a problem down.

244
00:10:07,330 --> 00:10:10,060
Junior developer B also uses AI actively.

245
00:10:10,330 --> 00:10:12,575
But they can read existing code, break down

246
00:10:12,590 --> 00:10:15,335
the problem, identify what to change,

247
00:10:15,350 --> 00:10:17,695
delegate the necessary work to AI, verify

248
00:10:17,710 --> 00:10:21,010
the result, and debug problems themselves.

249
00:10:21,733 --> 00:10:24,998
The company is not deciding between someone

250
00:10:25,013 --> 00:10:27,863
who uses AI and someone who does not.

251
00:10:28,093 --> 00:10:29,858
The more important difference going forward

252
00:10:29,873 --> 00:10:32,518
may be between someone who depends on

253
00:10:32,533 --> 00:10:34,293
AI and someone who uses it as a tool.

254
00:10:35,017 --> 00:10:37,142
In the past, companies could give junior

255
00:10:37,157 --> 00:10:38,907
developers relatively small tasks.

256
00:10:39,257 --> 00:10:40,767
Build this UI.

257
00:10:40,997 --> 00:10:42,527
Parse this data.

258
00:10:42,957 --> 00:10:44,427
Implement this class.

259
00:10:44,537 --> 00:10:46,547
Write this repetitive code.

260
00:10:46,737 --> 00:10:48,522
Through these tasks, juniors gained

261
00:10:48,537 --> 00:10:49,887
experience with a real codebase.

262
00:10:50,137 --> 00:10:51,967
Senior colleagues reviewed their code.

263
00:10:52,257 --> 00:10:53,167
They made mistakes.

264
00:10:54,383 --> 00:10:57,548
They also debugged, gradually growing into developers

265
00:10:57,563 --> 00:11:00,888
who could take responsibility for larger systems.

266
00:11:00,903 --> 00:11:04,748
Then AI arrived. Experienced developers can now

267
00:11:04,763 --> 00:11:07,828
use AI to complete some of the simple implementation

268
00:11:07,843 --> 00:11:10,193
work once assigned to juniors much faster.

269
00:11:10,623 --> 00:11:12,828
From the company's perspective, some of those small tasks

270
00:11:12,843 --> 00:11:15,533
that could be given to juniors may therefore shrink.

271
00:11:15,783 --> 00:11:18,528
I think this is an extremely important problem

272
00:11:18,543 --> 00:11:20,783
for junior developers in the age of AI.

273
00:11:21,517 --> 00:11:24,542
Before AI eliminates developers, it is reducing

274
00:11:24,557 --> 00:11:27,302
some of the first steps that juniors used to take

275
00:11:27,317 --> 00:11:29,627
on the way to becoming experienced developers.

276
00:11:29,877 --> 00:11:31,882
Paradoxically, the starting level expected

277
00:11:31,897 --> 00:11:34,157
of a junior may rise as a result.

278
00:11:34,883 --> 00:11:37,688
Here is something I particularly want to tell

279
00:11:37,703 --> 00:11:39,753
first- and second-year computer science students.

280
00:11:40,003 --> 00:11:42,428
During those years, try to build programs

281
00:11:42,443 --> 00:11:44,853
yourself without AI whenever possible.

282
00:11:45,063 --> 00:11:46,788
But the meaning of "build

283
00:11:46,803 --> 00:11:49,173
it yourself" matters here.

284
00:11:49,403 --> 00:11:51,568
Simply copying code from a book

285
00:11:51,583 --> 00:11:53,388
instead of looking at AI-generated

286
00:11:53,403 --> 00:11:55,443
code is not the practice I mean.

287
00:11:56,167 --> 00:11:57,752
Finding someone else's code

288
00:11:57,767 --> 00:11:59,337
online and copying it.

289
00:11:59,527 --> 00:12:01,772
Watching a YouTube tutorial and typing

290
00:12:01,787 --> 00:12:03,317
exactly what the instructor types.

291
00:12:03,587 --> 00:12:05,712
Keeping a finished example from a

292
00:12:05,727 --> 00:12:07,337
book open beside you and copying it.

293
00:12:07,627 --> 00:12:09,512
These are also different from producing

294
00:12:09,527 --> 00:12:11,237
a program from a blank project.

295
00:12:11,507 --> 00:12:13,937
Of course, I am not saying this kind of study is bad.

296
00:12:14,207 --> 00:12:16,157
When learning a concept for the first time, you need to read books.

297
00:12:17,050 --> 00:12:19,675
You need to watch lessons and read plenty of

298
00:12:19,690 --> 00:12:22,475
good code. Asking AI about a concept can also

299
00:12:22,490 --> 00:12:25,195
help. But you must distinguish practice in

300
00:12:25,210 --> 00:12:27,195
reading and understanding code from practice

301
00:12:27,210 --> 00:12:29,850
in producing code when you start with nothing.

302
00:12:30,567 --> 00:12:32,152
Suppose you are

303
00:12:32,167 --> 00:12:33,457
studying linked lists.

304
00:12:33,587 --> 00:12:35,017
First you read a book.

305
00:12:35,147 --> 00:12:36,737
You learn what a Node is.

306
00:12:37,007 --> 00:12:38,232
You learn how a pointer

307
00:12:38,247 --> 00:12:39,957
points to the next node.

308
00:12:40,167 --> 00:12:42,337
You study the principles of insertion and deletion.

309
00:12:42,507 --> 00:12:44,717
Of course you need references up to this point.

310
00:12:44,887 --> 00:12:47,017
But once you have finished studying, close the book.

311
00:12:47,167 --> 00:12:48,172
Turn off the internet.

312
00:12:48,187 --> 00:12:49,357
Turn off AI.

313
00:12:49,527 --> 00:12:50,937
Close the example code too.

314
00:12:51,187 --> 00:12:53,257
Then create an empty project.

315
00:12:53,307 --> 00:12:55,997
Start with nothing on the screen.

316
00:12:56,407 --> 00:12:58,327
"All right, let us build a linked list."

317
00:12:59,050 --> 00:13:01,895
At first, you might get stuck on "How did

318
00:13:01,910 --> 00:13:04,815
I declare a Node?" But that very moment

319
00:13:04,830 --> 00:13:07,255
matters. The part where you get stuck is

320
00:13:07,270 --> 00:13:09,290
the part that has not yet become your own.

321
00:13:10,017 --> 00:13:12,502
Suppose you followed a YouTube tutorial

322
00:13:12,517 --> 00:13:14,427
on making Tetris from beginning to end.

323
00:13:14,597 --> 00:13:17,082
In the video, the instructor creates

324
00:13:17,097 --> 00:13:19,502
the Board and Block, then implements

325
00:13:19,517 --> 00:13:22,107
Collision, Rotation, and Line Clear.

326
00:13:22,257 --> 00:13:24,027
The student follows along exactly.

327
00:13:24,257 --> 00:13:26,387
At the end, Tetris runs.

328
00:13:26,537 --> 00:13:28,682
But there is one more thing to try.

329
00:13:28,697 --> 00:13:29,782
Close the project.

330
00:13:29,797 --> 00:13:30,707
Turn off the lesson.

331
00:13:31,467 --> 00:13:32,832
Do not look at the source code either.

332
00:13:32,847 --> 00:13:35,477
Create a new, empty project.

333
00:13:35,947 --> 00:13:38,817
Now try making Tetris again.

334
00:13:39,007 --> 00:13:41,357
Suddenly, it becomes a completely different problem.

335
00:13:41,507 --> 00:13:44,817
"What should I build first?" "Where should I store the block

336
00:13:44,967 --> 00:13:47,992
data?" "Should the board use a two-dimensional array?" "When

337
00:13:48,007 --> 00:13:50,772
should I check collisions?" "How should I rotate a block?"

338
00:13:50,787 --> 00:13:52,972
"In what order should the game loop run?" I have

339
00:13:52,987 --> 00:13:55,277
to answer these questions, not the instructor.

340
00:13:55,527 --> 00:13:57,947
This is the process I mean by coding practice.

341
00:13:58,667 --> 00:14:01,912
This is something I especially want to emphasize to first-

342
00:14:01,927 --> 00:14:04,972
and second-year computer science students. During those

343
00:14:04,987 --> 00:14:08,412
years, turn off AI and, if possible, avoid looking at

344
00:14:08,427 --> 00:14:11,717
solution code. Build a program yourself from a blank project.

345
00:14:11,927 --> 00:14:13,152
The meaning of "build it

346
00:14:13,167 --> 00:14:15,557
yourself" matters here.

347
00:14:15,967 --> 00:14:17,592
I am not only talking about following

348
00:14:17,607 --> 00:14:20,027
along with AI-generated code.

349
00:14:20,750 --> 00:14:23,395
Finding someone else's code online and copying it.

350
00:14:23,410 --> 00:14:26,715
Watching a YouTube lesson and typing exactly what

351
00:14:26,730 --> 00:14:29,615
the instructor writes. Keeping a completed example

352
00:14:29,630 --> 00:14:32,335
from a book open beside you and copying it.

353
00:14:32,350 --> 00:14:34,575
These are also different from the

354
00:14:34,590 --> 00:14:36,800
blank-project practice I am discussing.

355
00:14:37,090 --> 00:14:38,635
Of course, these study methods

356
00:14:38,650 --> 00:14:40,480
are not inherently bad.

357
00:14:40,710 --> 00:14:42,755
When first learning a concept, you need

358
00:14:42,770 --> 00:14:45,155
examples from books, lessons, and plenty

359
00:14:45,170 --> 00:14:47,230
of good code written by other developers.

360
00:14:47,950 --> 00:14:50,375
But we need to distinguish practice in reading

361
00:14:50,390 --> 00:14:53,075
and understanding code from practice in

362
00:14:53,090 --> 00:14:54,990
creating code when we start with nothing.

363
00:14:55,717 --> 00:14:57,582
Suppose you are

364
00:14:57,597 --> 00:14:59,067
studying linked lists.

365
00:14:59,237 --> 00:15:00,747
First you read a book.

366
00:15:00,897 --> 00:15:03,022
You learn what a Node is, how a

367
00:15:03,037 --> 00:15:06,022
pointer points to the next node, and

368
00:15:06,037 --> 00:15:09,147
how insertion and deletion work.

369
00:15:09,417 --> 00:15:11,647
Of course, you need references up to this point.

370
00:15:11,917 --> 00:15:14,267
But once you have finished studying, close the book.

371
00:15:14,377 --> 00:15:17,927
Turn off the internet and AI, and close the example code.

372
00:15:18,197 --> 00:15:20,597
Then create an empty project.

373
00:15:21,317 --> 00:15:23,582
This is all that is on the screen.

374
00:15:23,597 --> 00:15:24,687
Now begin.

375
00:15:24,797 --> 00:15:27,347
Build a linked list yourself.

376
00:15:27,417 --> 00:15:29,665
At first, you might get stuck on "How did I create a Node?"

377
00:15:29,680 --> 00:15:32,402
That is okay. The very moment

378
00:15:32,417 --> 00:15:34,562
you get stuck reveals a part

379
00:15:34,577 --> 00:15:36,837
you have not yet made your own.

380
00:15:37,567 --> 00:15:39,872
Suppose you followed a YouTube tutorial

381
00:15:39,887 --> 00:15:41,737
on making Tetris from beginning to end.

382
00:15:41,927 --> 00:15:44,412
In the video, the instructor creates

383
00:15:44,427 --> 00:15:46,832
the Board and Block, then implements

384
00:15:46,847 --> 00:15:49,437
Collision, Rotation, and Line Clear.

385
00:15:49,587 --> 00:15:51,357
The student follows along exactly.

386
00:15:51,607 --> 00:15:53,717
At the end, Tetris runs.

387
00:15:53,867 --> 00:15:56,012
But there is one more thing to try.

388
00:15:56,027 --> 00:15:57,112
Close the project.

389
00:15:57,127 --> 00:15:58,057
Turn off the lesson.

390
00:15:58,783 --> 00:16:00,208
Do not look at the source code either.

391
00:16:00,223 --> 00:16:02,713
Create a new, empty project.

392
00:16:02,903 --> 00:16:05,593
Now try making Tetris again.

393
00:16:05,763 --> 00:16:08,413
Suddenly, it becomes a completely different problem.

394
00:16:08,583 --> 00:16:11,693
"What should I build first?" "Where should I store the block

395
00:16:11,903 --> 00:16:15,048
data?" "Should the board use a two-dimensional array?" "When

396
00:16:15,063 --> 00:16:18,008
should I check collisions?" "How should I rotate a block?"

397
00:16:18,023 --> 00:16:20,528
"In what order should the game loop run?" I have

398
00:16:20,543 --> 00:16:22,973
to answer these questions, not the instructor.

399
00:16:23,143 --> 00:16:25,983
This is the process I mean by coding practice.

400
00:16:26,700 --> 00:16:29,625
I consider this distinction extremely important.

401
00:16:29,640 --> 00:16:32,345
Reading code produced by AI and thinking, "Oh, that

402
00:16:32,360 --> 00:16:34,765
is how to do it." Reading a book's example and thinking,

403
00:16:34,780 --> 00:16:37,025
"Oh, that is how a linked list is implemented."

404
00:16:37,040 --> 00:16:39,385
Looking at someone's GitHub code and

405
00:16:39,400 --> 00:16:42,025
thinking, "So that is the structure they

406
00:16:42,040 --> 00:16:44,530
used." This kind of study is necessary too.

407
00:16:44,900 --> 00:16:46,910
But repeating only that mainly trains

408
00:16:47,160 --> 00:16:49,810
Recognition: recognizing what you see.

409
00:16:50,120 --> 00:16:52,710
Making a program from a blank project is different.

410
00:16:53,583 --> 00:16:55,353
The answer is not in front of you.

411
00:16:55,603 --> 00:16:58,648
You have to remember, break down the problem,

412
00:16:58,663 --> 00:17:01,728
choose the data structures, create functions and

413
00:17:01,743 --> 00:17:04,973
classes, and decide the implementation order.

414
00:17:05,243 --> 00:17:07,533
And when something is wrong, you have to find the cause.

415
00:17:07,723 --> 00:17:09,488
This is closer to practicing Recall

416
00:17:09,503 --> 00:17:11,583
and Problem Solving together.

417
00:17:12,300 --> 00:17:14,285
Suppose you have read 1,000 English sentences. You can

418
00:17:14,300 --> 00:17:17,265
understand all of them when you see them. But close

419
00:17:17,280 --> 00:17:20,185
the book and sit down in front of an English speaker,

420
00:17:20,200 --> 00:17:22,010
and suddenly you may find that nothing comes out.

421
00:17:22,380 --> 00:17:25,345
Understanding English that you see and producing sentences

422
00:17:25,360 --> 00:17:28,270
to express your own thoughts require different practice.

423
00:17:28,520 --> 00:17:29,905
Programming is similar.

424
00:17:29,920 --> 00:17:32,045
Reading linked list code ten times and

425
00:17:32,060 --> 00:17:34,065
building a linked list from scratch

426
00:17:34,080 --> 00:17:36,490
ten times are not the same practice.

427
00:17:37,350 --> 00:17:39,315
Understanding Tetris code and creating

428
00:17:39,330 --> 00:17:41,475
its structure yourself in an empty

429
00:17:41,490 --> 00:17:43,720
project are not the same practice either.

430
00:17:43,950 --> 00:17:45,475
So the study method I

431
00:17:45,490 --> 00:17:47,500
recommend is simple.

432
00:17:47,650 --> 00:17:48,815
Look at materials when you learn.

433
00:17:48,830 --> 00:17:50,375
Read books, watch lessons, and

434
00:17:50,390 --> 00:17:52,560
listen to AI's explanations.

435
00:17:52,790 --> 00:17:55,355
But once you understand reasonably well, close everything.

436
00:17:55,370 --> 00:17:57,590
Then build it again from a blank project.

437
00:17:58,317 --> 00:18:00,147
There is one important point here.

438
00:18:00,197 --> 00:18:02,327
You will naturally get stuck when starting from a blank project.

439
00:18:02,377 --> 00:18:03,567
That is normal.

440
00:18:03,897 --> 00:18:06,407
You might not even remember how to declare a Node.

441
00:18:06,557 --> 00:18:08,307
Pointers may confuse you.

442
00:18:08,537 --> 00:18:10,122
You might think for a long time

443
00:18:10,137 --> 00:18:11,887
about collision detection.

444
00:18:12,037 --> 00:18:14,957
You might spend two hours unable to find a single bug.

445
00:18:23,317 --> 00:18:25,442
But I think that time is extremely important. A

446
00:18:25,457 --> 00:18:28,702
problem you could solve in ten seconds with the

447
00:18:28,717 --> 00:18:31,682
solution beside you becomes something you think about

448
00:18:31,697 --> 00:18:34,622
for thirty minutes. You change code, set a breakpoint,

449
00:18:34,637 --> 00:18:37,527
inspect variables, fail, and build it again.

450
00:18:37,797 --> 00:18:40,867
That process trains your ability to turn problems into code.

451
00:18:41,157 --> 00:18:43,182
If the goal is to finish an assignment

452
00:18:43,197 --> 00:18:46,107
quickly, using AI is much more efficient.

453
00:18:46,137 --> 00:18:48,462
But if the goal is to learn

454
00:18:48,477 --> 00:18:50,882
programming, the fastest path may

455
00:18:50,897 --> 00:18:52,917
not always be the best way to learn.

456
00:18:53,650 --> 00:18:57,055
For first- and second-year university students, I

457
00:18:57,070 --> 00:18:59,660
recommend deliberately making plenty of time for this.

458
00:18:59,830 --> 00:19:03,400
After studying a data structure, close the solution and implement it yourself.

459
00:19:03,710 --> 00:19:06,980
After studying an algorithm, close the explanation and solve it again.

460
00:19:07,170 --> 00:19:09,615
After studying game programming, finish the

461
00:19:09,630 --> 00:19:12,240
lesson and rebuild it in an empty project.

462
00:19:12,470 --> 00:19:14,375
If possible, avoid immediately

463
00:19:14,390 --> 00:19:16,180
asking AI for the solution code.

464
00:19:16,330 --> 00:19:18,395
Give yourself enough time to think

465
00:19:18,410 --> 00:19:19,695
and produce code from scratch.

466
00:19:19,710 --> 00:19:23,235
On the other hand, once you have built basic programming

467
00:19:23,250 --> 00:19:26,300
proficiency, I think it is fine to use AI actively.

468
00:19:27,100 --> 00:19:30,085
As your projects grow in your third and

469
00:19:30,100 --> 00:19:32,650
fourth years, use AI to improve productivity.

470
00:19:32,840 --> 00:19:35,265
Use it to generate repetitive code, investigate

471
00:19:35,280 --> 00:19:38,225
libraries, write tests, get refactoring ideas,

472
00:19:38,240 --> 00:19:41,090
and quickly learn unfamiliar technologies.

473
00:19:41,120 --> 00:19:43,405
Once you enter the workplace, AI can

474
00:19:43,420 --> 00:19:45,630
be a powerful productivity tool.

475
00:19:45,900 --> 00:19:47,425
My message is not

476
00:19:47,440 --> 00:19:49,145
"Do not use AI."

477
00:19:49,160 --> 00:19:51,225
It is "Do not outsource the thinking process

478
00:19:51,240 --> 00:19:53,810
while you are learning programming."

479
00:19:54,550 --> 00:19:57,400
The goal is not to become someone who develops

480
00:19:57,470 --> 00:19:59,780
without AI. Nor is it to become a developer who

481
00:19:59,930 --> 00:20:02,440
never reads books or searches for information.

482
00:20:02,630 --> 00:20:05,435
At work, of course we read documentation

483
00:20:05,450 --> 00:20:07,095
and existing code, consult Stack Overflow,

484
00:20:07,110 --> 00:20:10,200
use AI, and ask colleagues.

485
00:20:10,410 --> 00:20:12,775
But while using those tools, the ability to turn a

486
00:20:12,790 --> 00:20:15,670
problem into a program must also exist in our own minds.

487
00:20:16,400 --> 00:20:19,665
That is why I hope computer science students spend

488
00:20:19,680 --> 00:20:22,865
at least their first two years gaining lots of

489
00:20:22,880 --> 00:20:25,090
experience building programs from blank projects.

490
00:20:25,300 --> 00:20:27,185
After learning, close the book.

491
00:20:27,200 --> 00:20:28,545
Turn off the lesson.

492
00:20:28,560 --> 00:20:29,865
Turn off AI too.

493
00:20:29,880 --> 00:20:31,510
Open an empty project.

494
00:20:31,860 --> 00:20:34,110
Start for yourself with nothing there.

495
00:20:34,420 --> 00:20:35,930
At first you will be very bad at it.

496
00:20:36,360 --> 00:20:38,985
But with repetition, at some point the structure of the code

497
00:20:39,000 --> 00:20:42,070
will begin to form in your mind as soon as you see a problem.

498
00:20:42,967 --> 00:20:45,292
Then try using AI again.

499
00:20:45,307 --> 00:20:48,037
From that point on, AI becomes a tool that

500
00:20:48,067 --> 00:20:50,072
implements your ideas much faster, rather

501
00:20:50,087 --> 00:20:52,137
than one that thinks in your place.

502
00:20:52,967 --> 00:20:56,037
If you want to develop a habit of designing and implementing programs

503
00:20:56,267 --> 00:21:00,077
yourself, explore YamyamCoding programming coaching and tutoring.

504
00:21:00,547 --> 00:21:03,612
The information is for learners seeking direction in

505
00:21:03,627 --> 00:21:05,997
breaking down and implementing game programming problems.

506
00:21:06,367 --> 00:21:08,892
The coaching and tutoring link will be in the description,

507
00:21:08,907 --> 00:21:11,477
at the end of the video, and in the pinned comment.

508
00:21:11,787 --> 00:21:14,397
Summarize what you are learning, the code

509
00:21:14,647 --> 00:21:16,957
you have made, and where you are stuck.

510
00:21:17,367 --> 00:21:18,712
Begin with a specific account

511
00:21:18,727 --> 00:21:20,217
of the help you need.
